\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Only the zero-momentum block survives the free Wilson term}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{Take the [Wilson operator](fdip-009U) at trivial links,
with #{a>0} and real #{r\ne0}, on the periodic grid with
#{N_j\ge3}. Its kernel consists exactly of constant fields.
In particular,
##{\dim_{\mathbb C}\ker D_{W,a,1}=2^d q.}
For the continuous momentum symbol, a corner having #{k}
coordinates equal to #{\pi} receives scalar Wilson term
#{2rk/a}; it is nonzero for #{k\ge1}.}

\proof{
\p{The [finite Fourier decomposition](fdip-009Q) has momenta
#{p_j=2\pi n_j/N_j}, with #{0\le n_j<N_j}. These are
dimensionless lattice momenta; their physical representatives
are #{p_j/a}. Put
##{\begin{aligned}
A_a(p)&=\frac{i}{a}\sum_j\Gamma_j\sin p_j,\\
M_a(p)&=\frac{r}{a}\sum_j(1-\cos p_j).
\end{aligned}}
The Wilson block is #{A_a(p)+M_a(p)I}. The self-adjoint
Clifford operators of \ref{fdip-00LZ}, and their anticommutation
relations, give
##{\begin{aligned}
A_a(p)^*&=-A_a(p),\\
A_a(p)^2&=-a^{-2}\sum_j\sin^2p_j\,I.
\end{aligned}}
Since #{M_a(p)} is real and scalar, the mixed terms cancel:
##{\begin{aligned}
&(A_a+M_aI)^*(A_a+M_aI)\\
&\qquad=\left(a^{-2}\sum_j\sin^2p_j+M_a^2\right)I.
\end{aligned}}
Thus a block has a nonzero kernel precisely when both
#{\sum_j\sin^2p_j=0} and #{M_a(p)=0}. Because
#{r\ne0} and each #{1-\cos p_j\ge0}, the latter condition
already forces every #{\cos p_j=1}. In the chosen momentum
range this means #{p=0}. Conversely the entire block at
#{p=0} is zero. Its dimension is #{\dim(S\otimes F)=2^d q},
and the zero Fourier character is the constant field.}

\p{The same formula is defined for every real momentum modulo
#{2\pi}. At #{p=\pi\epsilon}, with
#{\epsilon\in\{0,1\}^d}, the sine part vanishes and
##{M_a(\pi\epsilon)=\frac{2r}{a}\sum_j\epsilon_j.}
This proves the corner claim, whether or not a particular finite
grid samples that corner exactly.}
}

\p{For #{q=1}, periods #{(4,4)} and #{(4,6)} both give
kernel dimension #{4}; periods #{(4,4,4)} give 8.
The same formula applies to odd periods: zero momentum is
always present and every other block is invertible.}

\p{The scalar addition is the free Wilson denominator mechanism
in \citet{Section 9.3, Equations (9.15)–(9.16), p. 21}{wingate2025lattice}.
For #{r>0}, its value at each nonzero corner grows as #{1/a}.
This is an exact free-symbol statement, not a continuum-limit
construction or an interacting mass calculation. The cancellation
above uses the scalar free block and proves no corresponding
kernel formula for a general connection. The surviving block
still contains the whole chosen reducible #{S\otimes F}.}

\p{Remark. Leap §13, rung 2, names the Kähler–Dirac operator
#{D=d+\delta} as the doubler-free comparison with this Wilson
term. That comparison is not proved here.}
